Mission Statement

"If you're here, you're a part of this thing."

The Never Alone Recovery Community is an independent community focused on connecting people to recovery resources. 

We provide a place where people can connect with one another between meetings and throughout their recovery journey. 

We hope that no one seeking recovery ever has to feel they have nowhere to turn.


History

This community was previously known through an earlier online chat space. 

Our original domain name remains active and will continue to bring visitors here. We ask that when sharing or linking to our community, you please use neveralonerc.org.


We maintain our own purpose while respecting the purpose of meetings

We recognize that many people who visit us are taking an important first step toward recovery. Whether they stay here, attend a meeting, or choose another recovery resource, we hope that first experience encourages them to continue seeking support.

Our community includes people from a variety of recovery fellowships, and we recognize that no single path works for everyone.

How We Connect Today

When the "NA Chatroom" began in the early 2000s, online recovery meetings were limited. As online meetings have become widely available, our role has evolved alongside them.

Visitors use our community in different ways. Some arrive looking for an online or in-person meeting. Others stay to talk with people in recovery, ask questions, or learn about available resources. Whether someone visits once or becomes part of the community, our goal is to help them take the next positive step.

We maintain an online meeting information page listing active NA and other 12-step meetings, with direct Zoom and phone access for those seeking immediate entry without chat or login.


Cooperative Structure

The Never Alone Recovery Community is being developed as a member-owned cooperative. The legal structure is still evolving, but we welcome input and suggestions from the community.

We are not governed by the Seventh Tradition, though we respect its principles of self-support and declining outside contributions.
